Thanks to David Smith from Insightful for his quick response.

### ORIGINAL POST ###
Could someone give me an example of what this statement means?

``Objects must be assigned to the working data directory before they can
be modified by replacement functions. Earlier versions of S-PLUS
automatically assigned the modified object to the working data.''

I found it in the Release Notes under ``Object-Oriented Programming''
but I'm unable to verify its implication.

### RESPONSE ###
David Smith wrote:
> 
> It means you cannot modify objects in databases other than the working
> database, other than by using assign() explicitly.  For example, the
> built-in object fuel.frame is found at position 4 of the search path:
> 
> > find("fuel.frame")
> [1] "data"
> 
> Attempting to modify it gives an error.
> 
> > fuel.frame$Weight <- fuel.frame$Weight+1
> Problem: object "fuel.frame" must be assigned locally before replacement
> 
> In older versions of S-PLUS, this would have first copied fuel.frame to
> the working chapter (database 1) and then replaced the Weight component.
> But in S-PLUS 6 you have to explicitly copy it first:
> 
> Use traceback() to see the call stack
> > fuel.frame <- fuel.frame
> > fuel.frame$Weight <- fuel.frame$Weight+1
> 
> # David Smith
