Dear S-PLUS mailing list,

I'm trying to write a function in order to fit a Poisson model to
Phytoplankton data, namely Pseudo-nitzschia's quantity. Afterwards, I want
to find a parsimonious subset of the independent variables, which are
related to the upwelling index lagged by 1 day, 2 days, etc, from the day
when the sample of Pseudo-nitzschia was collected. This procedure has to be
repeated several times, so I decided to write my own function.

I have done the following instructions:

"Poisson.Adjustment"<-function(data.phyto)
{
        (....) 

adjust.phyto<-
glm(specie.phyto ~ . , family=robust(poisson,maxit=30),data=data.phyto)

adjust.phyto.step<-
step.glm(adjust.phyto,scope=list(upper= ~ . ,lower= ~ upwell0days))

        (....)
}

When I call Poisson.Adjustment function in the Command Window, it returns
the following error message:

Error: Object "data.phyto" not found

I've already tried to call the "step.glm" function with no reference to the
"scope" argument, but the error message remains the same.

After deleting the line corresponding to the stepwise procedure, all works
very well including the recognition of the data.frame called "data.phyto".

I don't understand why the inclusion of a calling to "step.glm" (or "step")
function gives raise to such confusion when running the function that I'd
written. Maybe once you'd been faced with a similar problem.

Thanks in advanced.

Helena Mouriño Nunes.

